\\(c_2\\), \\(c_1\\), and \\(c_0\\) in the equation\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0,\\]\nwe will expand the right-hand side and then compare coefficients with the left-hand side.\n\nFirst, expand \\((x-3)^3\\):\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27.\n\\]\nNow, let's expand the full expression on the right-hand side:\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 + c_2 (x-3)^2 + c_1 (x-3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 (x^2 - 6x + 9) + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0.\n\\]\nSimplify the expression by distributing and combining like terms:\n\\[\nx^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2 + c_1 x - 3c_1 + c_0 = x^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\n\\]\nWe now compare the coefficients of the expanded form with the original polynomial \\(x^3 + x^2 - 5\\):\n\\[\nx^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0) = x^3 + x^2 - 5.\n\\]\nBy comparing the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n1. \\(c_2 - 9 = 1\\),\n2. \\(27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1\\),\n3. \\(-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"aa9c49e87eba0103160c1fb8239c64168349a7d7f641cc98356df0ae6cd9579c"},"seconds":54.323627948760986,"seed":595316365747877192,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the constants \\(c_2\\), \\(c_1\\), and \\(c_0\\) in the equation\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0,\\]\nwe will expand the right-hand side and then compare coefficients with the left-hand side.\n\nFirst, expand \\((x-3)^3\\):\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27.\n\\]\nNext, expand \\(c_2 (x-3)^2\\):\n\\[\nc_2 (x-3)^2 = c_2 (x^2 - 6x + 9) = c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2.\n\\]\nThen, expand \\(c_1 (x-3)\\):\n\\[\nc_1 (x-3) = c_1 x - 3c_1.\n\\]\nSo, the right-hand side becomes:\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 + c_2 (x-3)^2 + c_1 (x-3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2 + c_1 x - 3c_1 + c_0.\n\\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\nx^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\n\\]\nWe compare this with the left-hand side \\(x^3 + x^2 - 5\\), which gives us the following system of equations:\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5.\n\\]\nSolving the first equation:\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1 \\implies c_2 = 10.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\n27 - 6(10) + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 60 + c_1 = 1 \\implies -33 + c_1 = 1 \\implies c_1 = 34.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) and \\(c_1 = 34\\) into the third equation:\n\\[\n-27 + 9(10) - 3(34) + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 90 - 102 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -39 + c_0 = -5 \\implies c_0 = 34.\n\\]\nThus, we have \\(c_2 = 10\\), \\(c_1 = 34\\), and \\(c_0 = 34\\). We now find \\(c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2\\):\n\\[\nc_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 34^2 + 34^2 = 100 + 1156 + 1156 = 2412.\n\\]\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2412}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2412}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"To solve for the constants \\(c_2\\), \\(c_1\\), and \\(c_0\\) in the equation\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0,\\]\nwe will expand the right-hand side and then compare coefficients with the left-hand side.\n\nFirst, expand \\((x-3)^3\\):\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27.\n\\]\nNext, expand \\(c_2 (x-3)^2\\):\n\\[\nc_2 (x-3)^2 = c_2 (x^2 - 6x + 9) = c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2.\n\\]\nThen, expand \\(c_1 (x-3)\\):\n\\[\nc_1 (x-3) = c_1 x - 3c_1.\n\\]\nSo, the right-hand side becomes:\n\\[\n(x-3)^3 + c_2 (x-3)^2 + c_1 (x-3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2 x^2 - 6c_2 x + 9c_2 + c_1 x - 3c_1 + c_0.\n\\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\nx^3 + (c_2 - 9)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\n\\]\nWe compare this with the left-hand side \\(x^3 + x^2 - 5\\), which gives us the following system of equations:\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n27 - 6c_2 + c_1 = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 = -5.\n\\]\nSolving the first equation:\n\\[\nc_2 - 9 = 1 \\implies c_2 = 10.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\n27 - 6(10) + c_1 = 1 \\implies 27 - 60 + c_1 = 1 \\implies -33 + c_1 = 1 \\implies c_1 = 34.\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(c_2 = 10\\) and \\(c_1 = 34\\) into the third equation:\n\\[\n-27 + 9(10) - 3(34) + c_0 = -5 \\implies -27 + 90 - 102 + c_0 = -5 \\implies -39 + c_0 = -5 \\implies c_0 = 34.\n\\]\nThus, we have \\(c_2 = 10\\), \\(c_1 = 34\\), and \\(c_0 = 34\\). We now find \\(c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2\\):\n\\[\nc_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 34^2 + 34^2 = 100 + 1156 + 1156 = 2412.\n\\]\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2412}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:417"}
